   "passage": "On a hot afternoon, the center of a large city can be several degrees warmer than the farmland just outside it. Scientists call this difference the urban heat island effect. It happens because cities replace soil and plants with asphalt, concrete, and dark rooftops, materials that absorb sunlight during the day and release the stored heat slowly at night. Air conditioners, vehicles, and factories add even more heat, while tall buildings block the wind that might otherwise carry __it__ away.\n\nThe consequences go beyond discomfort. During heat waves, residents of the hottest neighborhoods are more likely to suffer from heat exhaustion, and older people and outdoor workers face the greatest risk. Higher temperatures also increase the demand for electricity, which, when that electricity comes from fossil fuels, releases more greenhouse gases and makes the problem worse.\n\nCity planners have begun to fight back with surprisingly simple tools. Painting roofs white or light gray reflects a large share of sunlight instead of absorbing it. Planting trees along streets provides shade and cools the air through evaporation from their leaves. Green roofs, which are covered with a thin layer of soil and plants, insulate the buildings beneath them and can lower roof surface temperatures dramatically on sunny days.\n\nNone of these measures is free, and they work best in combination. A single park will not cool a whole district, and reflective roofs help little if the streets around them remain bare. Still, cities that have invested consistently in trees and cooler surfaces report fewer heat-related hospital visits. For rapidly growing cities in tropical countries, where temperatures are already high, such investments may soon become a necessity rather than a choice.",
