   "title": "Text 2",
   "passage": "For decades, teenagers who struggled to get up for early classes were simply called lazy. Research on the body clock now suggests a different explanation. During puberty, the brain begins releasing melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, about one to two hours later than it did in childhood. As a result, many adolescents genuinely cannot fall asleep before eleven o'clock at night, no matter how early they go to bed.\n\nThis shift would matter little if school started later, but most schools begin early in the morning. A student who falls asleep at eleven and wakes at five-thirty gets six and a half hours of sleep, well below the eight to ten hours that sleep experts recommend for this age group. The loss accumulates over the week. By Friday, many students are carrying a significant sleep debt, which they try to repay by sleeping late on weekends. Unfortunately, this habit pushes their body clock even later, making Monday morning even harder.\n\nThe cost is not only tiredness. Sleep is when the brain __consolidates__ memories, transferring what was learned during the day into long-term storage. Students who are short of sleep tend to remember less of what they studied, have more difficulty concentrating, and are more irritable. Some studies have also linked chronic sleep loss in teenagers to a higher risk of accidents and low mood.\n\nSeveral school districts that moved their start times later reported that students slept longer and attended class more regularly. Critics point out that later schedules can complicate transportation and after-school activities. Yet for students themselves, a few practical habits can help: keeping a consistent wake-up time every day, avoiding bright screens before bed, and getting morning sunlight, which helps reset the body clock.",
