   "passage": "Along much of Indonesia's coastline, forests grow where few trees can survive: in salty, muddy water that floods twice a day with the tides. These are mangroves, and Indonesia has more of them than any other country. For a long time they were seen as useless swamps, and large areas were cleared to make room for shrimp ponds, farms, and ports.\n\nScientists now value mangroves far more highly. Their tangled roots slow down waves and trap sediment, protecting coastal villages from storms and erosion. Their shallow waters serve as nurseries for many species of fish and crabs that local fishers depend on. Most importantly for the climate, mangroves are extraordinarily efficient at storing carbon. Because their __waterlogged__ soil contains little oxygen, dead leaves and roots decompose very slowly, so carbon builds up in the mud over centuries. A hectare of mangrove can hold several times more carbon than a hectare of tropical forest on dry land. Carbon stored in coastal ecosystems like this is known as \"blue carbon.\"\n\nThe danger is that this process also works in reverse. When a mangrove forest is cut down and its soil is dug up for ponds, the carbon that accumulated over hundreds of years is exposed to the air and released as carbon dioxide within a short period.\n\nFor this reason, many researchers argue that protecting existing mangroves is more urgent than planting new ones. Replanting projects are valuable, but young seedlings often die when they are planted in the wrong places, and even successful plantings take decades to store as much carbon as a mature forest.",
