   "title": "Text 4",
   "passage": "In recent years, a growing number of schools around the world have banned students from using smartphones during the school day. Supporters of these bans argue that phones distract students in class, encourage cyberbullying, and reduce face-to-face interaction during breaks. Some schools that introduced bans have reported calmer classrooms and more conversation in the canteen.\n\nThese benefits are real, but I believe a complete ban is the wrong answer. First, the evidence is less clear than headlines suggest. Several large studies have found only small differences in test scores between schools with and without bans, and it is difficult to separate the effect of a ban from other changes the schools made at the same time. Second, smartphones are now basic tools for learning. Students use them to look up unfamiliar words, check scientific data, and collaborate on projects. Removing them entirely treats a useful tool as if it were only a toy.\n\nMost importantly, a ban avoids the real problem rather than solving it. When students graduate, no one will take their phones away. They will have to decide for themselves when to focus and when to scroll. School is exactly the place where they should practice that skill, with teachers guiding them.\n\nA better approach is a clear set of rules: phones stay in bags during lessons unless the teacher asks students to use __them__, and they are allowed during certain break times. Schools can also teach students how apps are designed to capture attention. Such a policy keeps the classroom focused while preparing young people for a world in which the phone is never far away.",
