   "title": "Text 5",
   "passage": "For centuries, the coast of South Sulawesi has been home to some of the most skilled boatbuilders in Southeast Asia. In villages such as Tana Beru in Bulukumba, craftsmen still build the pinisi, a large wooden sailing ship with two masts and seven sails, using methods passed down from generation to generation. In 2017, UNESCO recognized the art of pinisi boatbuilding as part of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.\n\nWhat makes the tradition remarkable is not only the ship itself but the way it is built. Unlike many shipbuilders elsewhere, pinisi builders traditionally do not work from written plans or drawings. The master builder, known as the panrita lopi, keeps the design in his head and directs the work from memory and experience. The hull is constructed first, plank by plank, and the internal frame is added afterward, the reverse of the order used in most European shipbuilding. Each stage, from cutting the first piece of the keel to launching the finished vessel, is accompanied by ceremonies and prayers that reflect the community's beliefs.\n\nToday the tradition faces new __pressures__. Suitable hardwood is becoming harder to find and more expensive, and many young people prefer jobs in cities. At the same time, demand has shifted. Fewer pinisi are built to carry cargo, while more are built as tourist cruise ships, which require engines, cabins, and modern safety equipment. Some builders worry that these changes may slowly transform the craft, but others see tourism as the very thing that keeps the skills alive.",
